1469:
An application for a parade permit shall be made to the city manager at least two (2) weeks prior to the proposed date of the parade and shall contain all necessary information relating to the parade including but in no way limited to:
(1) The name of the person sponsoring and/or conducting the parade;
(2) The purpose of the parade;
(3) The date and beginning and ending hours of the parade;
(4) The location or proposed route in the city of the parade;
(5) The estimated number of participants expected to participate in the parade, including the number and type of vehicles, marching units, or floats; and
(6) The name, address, and telephone number of a contact person with the sponsor of the parade who will be familiar with all aspects of the parade.
(Ord. No. 90-4, § 4, 2-13-90)

1473:
The city manager shall issue a permit as provided herein when, from consideration of the application and such other information as may be otherwise available, he finds that:
(1) The conduct of the parade will not substantially interfere with or interrupt the safe and orderly movement of other traffic along or contiguous with the route of the parade;
(2) The conduct of the parade will not require the diversion of so great a number of police or other personnel of the city to properly police and regulate the parade and the areas contiguous thereto so as to prevent or interfere substantially with the other activities of the city or prevent normal police protection in the city;
(3) The conduct of the parade will not substantially interfere with or prevent normal ambulance services to portions of the city other than the proposed route of the parade and areas contiguous thereto;
(4) The concentration of persons, animals, vehicles, floats, or other parts of the parade will not unduly interfere with proper fire and police protection of or ambulance service to areas contiguous to the assembly areas;
(5) The conduct of the parade will not interfere substantially with the movement of fire-fighting equipment enroute to a fire;
(6) The parade route is reasonably fixed and scheduled so as to move the parade and its participants from its point of origin to its point of termination expeditiously and without unreasonable delays enroute; and
(7) The parade is not to be held solely for the purpose of advertising any product, goods, or event and is not designed or calculated to be held purely for private purposes.
(Ord. No. 90-4, § 8, 2-13-90)

